    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive profile, anchored by **Trea Turner** (79.76) and **Kyle Schwarber** (78.41). Both occupy the top of the order and benefit from a premier matchup against Gilbert, whose 5.89 xERA provides an ideal environment for our leadoff and two-hole hitters to maximize run production. **Junior Caminero** (72.40) also stands out as an elite play; despite a slight park penalty, his strong SP skill adjustment and cleanup duties against Alcantara make him a primary engine for RBI production. We are heavily favoring left-handed bats against today\u2019s right-handed starters, with **Lars Nootbaar** (65.90) and **Gary Sanchez** (65.04) leveraging platoon advantages to solidify their status as high-efficiency starts.\n\nOur most difficult lineup decision today involved the final utility spot, where **Kazuma Okamoto** (53.48) narrowly held off **Caleb Durbin** (47.00). While **Caleb Durbin** brings essential speed that we value highly in our 5x5 framework, the projection model favored **Kazuma Okamoto**'s power potential against Baz, despite a slight downgrade for hitting lower in the order. We are maintaining discipline with our bench; **Pedro Ramirez** (34.42) was excluded entirely as he fell below our 40-point \"Zebras Floor,\" ensuring we preserve game caps for higher-leverage situations.\n\nRisks remain, particularly with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (60.83). While he projects as a strong start, the matchup against McDonald (3.14 xERA) is daunting, and wind blowing in at the stadium introduces significant volatility that could suppress his scoring ceiling. Similarly, we are monitoring **Yordan Alvarez** (60.16) closely; although his raw xwOBA is elite, the platoon disadvantage against a left-handed pitcher creates a sub-optimal environment that drags down his overall efficiency. We will be watching for late lineup changes to ensure our aggressive stacking against lower-tier starters remains fully intact.",
